= 1
{parent=Paper 17}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $F$ be the generator of the <smooth flow>. The <Anosov flow> property requires $F$ to be nowhere zero and a continuous splitting of the <tangent bundle>
$$
TN=E^s\oplus\mathbb RF\oplus E^u
$$
which is invariant under $D\phi_t$. There must be constants $C\ge1$ and $\kappa>0$ such that, for every $x$, every $t\ge0$ and every vector in the indicated fibre,
$$
\boxed{\|D\phi_t\xi_s\|\le Ce^{-\kappa t}\|\xi_s\|,
\qquad
\|D\phi_{-t}\xi_u\|\le Ce^{-\kappa t}\|\xi_u\|.}
$$
The first inequality defines the <Anosov stable bundle>; the second defines the <Anosov unstable bundle> and is equivalent to uniform forward expansion there. The remaining one-dimensional direction is the flow direction. The <norm> can come from any <Riemannian metric>, since norms are uniformly equivalent on a <closed manifold>. The essential requirements are a continuous invariant splitting and estimates uniform in both the base point and time, rather than separate exponential rates along individual orbits.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here is the <quadratic-form criterion for an Anosov flow>. Assume that the generator $F$ is nowhere zero and form the transverse <vector bundle>
$$
\mathcal E=TN/\mathbb RF.
$$
Since $D\phi_tF(x)=F(\phi_t x)$, the derivative induces a linear cocycle $P_t:\mathcal E_x\to\mathcal E_{\phi_t x}$. Suppose there is a continuous nondegenerate indefinite <quadratic form> $Q_x$ on each fibre, with constant <signature of a quadratic form>, such that $t\mapsto Q_{\phi_t x}(P_t\xi)$ is differentiable and
$$
\mathcal DQ_x(\xi)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}Q_{\phi_t x}(P_t\xi)
$$
is continuous and **positive definite on every transverse fibre**. Then $\phi_t$ is an <Anosov flow>. An equivalent way to specify $Q$ is a <quadratic form> on $TN$ whose radical is exactly $\mathbb RF$ and whose induced form and derivative satisfy these conditions on the quotient.

On the <closed manifold>, compactness makes the positivity uniform: for some $c>0$, $\mathcal DQ_x(\xi)\ge c\|\xi\|^2$. Thus the positive and negative cones are strictly separated by the linearized flow, yielding uniform unstable and stable transverse subbundles and their invariant lifts. The positive index is the unstable dimension and the negative index is the stable dimension. Strict positivity must be imposed transversely; imposing it on the flow direction would be impossible because that direction is carried into itself.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the <Levi-Civita connection> to represent a tangent vector to the <unit tangent bundle> at $(x,v)$ by the initial data $(J(0),D_tJ(0))$ of the <Jacobi field> generated by a variation of the unit-speed <geodesic>. The unit-speed constraint gives $\langle D_tJ(0),v\rangle=0$. Along the <geodesic>, write
$$
J(t)=a\dot\gamma(t)+y(t)i\dot\gamma(t),\qquad D_tJ(t)=\dot y(t)i\dot\gamma(t).
$$
Here $a$ is constant, while the perpendicular <Jacobi field> equation is
$$
\ddot y+K(\gamma(t))y=0.
$$
The flow direction is represented by $(a,y,\dot y)=(1,0,0)$. Consequently $(y,z)=(y,\dot y)$ are coordinates on the transverse quotient. Define its nondegenerate <quadratic form> by $Q(y,z)=yz$. Its <signature of a quadratic form> is $(1,1)$, and
$$
\frac{d}{dt}Q(y(t),z(t))=z(t)^2-K(\gamma(t))y(t)^2.
$$
Since the <Gaussian curvature> is strictly negative and the surface is a <closed manifold>, there is $k_0>0$ with $K\le-k_0$. Hence
$$
\dot Q\ge\min(1,k_0)(y^2+z^2).
$$
The <quadratic-form criterion for an Anosov flow> now proves **the geodesic flow is Anosov**. This is the <geodesic hyperbolicity from negative Gaussian curvature> argument. If the surface is not oriented, the local choice of perpendicular unit vector changes both $y$ and $z$ in sign; their product is unchanged, so the <quadratic form> still exists globally.

When $K=-1$, the perpendicular solutions are $y=Ae^t+Be^{-t}$. The pure contracting data have $(y,z)=(B,-B)$ and the pure expanding data have $(y,z)=(A,A)$. In the <canonical coframe of a surface unit tangent bundle>, $H$ represents $(iv,0)$ and $V$ represents $(0,iv)$, so
$$
\boxed{E^s(x,v)=\mathbb R(H-V),\qquad E^u(x,v)=\mathbb R(H+V).}
$$
Equivalently the <Anosov stable bundle> consists of pairs $(\eta,-\eta)$ with $\eta\perp v$, and the <Anosov unstable bundle> consists of $(\eta,\eta)$. The derivative multiplies these data by $e^{-t}$ and $e^t$, respectively, using parallel transport along the <geodesic> to identify the perpendicular directions. Together with $\mathbb RX$ they give the required invariant splitting.

